{
  "case_id": "2020-20-297",
  "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "TransUnion LLC",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The class certified here suffers from two fatal defects: the absence of class member standing and typicality."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "TransUnion LLC",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But typicality means something different from commonality, and the typicality requirement precludes a class representative with wholly atypical injuries."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "TransUnion LLC",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: Justice Thomas, I think that that client would have standing to sue because I think contracts are different for the following reason: Just by virtue of having a contract action, I think that means that you gave consideration in exchange for the promise."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is it --is it that there's no material risk that these people faced, or is it that they didn't know about it? Which is the key to you, your argument in response to Justice Kagan?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0001-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Spokeo, this Court discussed a number of considerations that are relevant to whether a violation of a statutory right constitutes a concrete injury, all of which point the same direction here. The class members have standing to bring reasonable procedures claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: So -- but, if there isn't an abuse of discretion, on what basis would we send it back?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. REAVES: Mr. Ramirez's injuries are atypical, we think. And, you know, a claim is not necessarily defined as just the elements that an individual needs to prove. Black's Law Dictionary, when it defines \"claim,\" includes the relief that's requested."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: In Spokeo, the opinion says, \"not all inaccuracies cause harm or present any material risk of harm.\" Do you read that as -- in -- as saying that there is injury-in-fact whenever there is material risk of harm? Do you read that as setting out a legal test for injury-in-fact?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "TransUnion",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "TransUnion created an explosively high risk of harm by placing OFAC designations not in the secretive draft -- desk drawer but in the readily acceptable credit files of innocent Americans."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Both courts below found that the claims asserted were the same for all class members, following the text of Rule 23(a)(3)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: Good morning, Mr. Issacharoff. I have a question about whether you can ever have a bare procedural violation with respect to any of these consumer protection statutes, like FCRA or the FDCPA."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0004-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "2012, which is still within the statutory period, within the class period, there were over 17,000 OFAC alerts sent out."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0004-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: So then is it your position that the reason why there was standing for these things coming in the two envelopes and the OFAC envelope not having the specific information that was included in the first credit report -- is it your position that the reason why that's not a bare procedural violation as opposed to something else --you didn't give an example, but something you say would be trivial -- is it because of the -- the -- it -- it being inherently shocking and confusing, like the Ninth Circuit said? Is that what distinguishes it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-297",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a89a2bad86e7235e91b480f0c5246ad320fa9312b3743532c198339e21f8145d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a89a2bad86e7-0004-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 94,
        "start": 94
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case was submitted."
    },
